<.shell current={:field_definitions}>
  <h1>Field definitions</h1>
  <p class="lead">What each custom field on a team's records is, never its values.</p>

  <h2>The list</h2>
  <div class="demo">
    <.field_list
      id="field-definitions-sample"
      fields={custom_field_definitions()}
      path={fn _field -> "#" end}
      publish?
    />
  </div>

  <h2>One field</h2>
  <div class="demo">
    <.field_details
      field={custom_field_definitions() |> List.last()}
      timezone="America/Vancouver"
      team_names={%{11 => "Coast Range SAR"}}
      organization_name="WESARA"
      restricted_text="Only team admins see its values."
    />
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    The Fields pages in settings, for a team or an organization (#872). The list is an index to scan: each field's
    name, the first line of what it means, and Needed to publish. The fields in no group come first. Then each named
    group of fields follows under its title, as on a record's Fields tab.
  </p>
  <p>
    The name opens the field's own page: what it means, its type, what it's on, who added it and when, and every
    choice, one per line. A choice a team added to its organization's field is listed under the team's name.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <p>
    A record's own values. Those are the record's Fields tab, a <a href={page_path(:detail_list)}>detail list</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.field_list&gt;</code>
    takes one kind's fields, with their groups preloaded, and a <code>path</code>
    function to each field's page. <code>copy?</code>
    adds who sees a member field and its Copy check box. <code>&lt;.field_details&gt;</code>
    is the field's own page.
  </p>
</.shell>
